if I'm certain of one thing in life it's  that there are cheap laptops and great  laptops but cheap laptops are never  great and great laptops are never cheap  that's become the Chromebook problem for  $250 you get good for the price web  focus laptops but they have flimsy  plastic that feels like a toy low  resolution washed out screens and  trackpads that make you want to throw  something at them but then there's  Google's very own high-end Chromebook  pixel which as you would expect for its  high price has none of those flaws and  today it's getting even better with a  bigger battery faster processor faster  charging technology and lots of other  new tricks I would have told you you  were insane to buy the first pixel which  came out two years ago all that money  for a laptop that just browses the web  but not anymore not only is the improved  pixels price down to a slightly more  affordable $1000 but these days it's  much easier to live in a web browser the  new pixel looks almost identical to the  old pixel and I'm actually fine with  that the solid aluminium design makes it  one of the most well crafted laptops  ever it looks great and feels even  better I have a serious crush on this  laptop the backlit keyboard is sturdy  the trackpad is incredibly responsive to  multi-touch gestures and the twelve  point eight inch high-res touchscreen is  crisp and incredibly bright but good  luck trying to use it outdoors  I do wish Google would have shaved off a  little bit of the thickness and weight  of the system especially considering it  has this new USB type-c port which is  much thinner the tiny socket charges up  the laptop really fast in a little more  than an hour  it also has bi-directional power so you  can use the port to charge other gadgets  the big excitement is that the plug is  reversible no more failed attempts to  plug in your USB cable upside down even  better you need to charge the pixel that  often the battery lasted over 11 hours  it also has this cool battery meter on  the lid yet the biggest difference  between this pixel and the pixel from  two years ago doesn't have really  anything to do with hardware it has to  do with the progression  the web and us Chrome OS is still pretty  much just a Chrome browser though with  some pretty cool tricks  but unlike a few years ago so many  desktop apps now have web versions still  many don't there's still no Skype or  iTunes many apps and websites won't work  when you don't have an internet  connection and peripheral support  especially printing is a major issue  then while it comes with one terabyte of  google drive space there's only 32  gigabytes of local storage that's why  the pixel or even a $250 Chromebook  still isn't for everyone  laptops like the Dell XPS 13 or Apple's  MacBook Air are more affordable and much  more practical if the pixel will price  at around $800 it would be a much  stronger alternative but if you've  decided that living in Chrome is right  for you then it's time to ponder this  deep question do you want a cheap  Chromebook or a great one
 

